  "initial_prompt": "Complete this single task yourself using the tools: Turn the cup so that the open side faces upward, then pour all the cookies into the bowl.\nRobot: manip/lift2/R5a, two arms and a movable planar base. Left arm is left in the overlook view. EEF poses are local to each arm's planner base: +X forward, +Y robot-left, +Z up; quaternion order wxyz. Fingers extend along tool +X and open along tool Y. Wrist cameras move/rotate with the hands. Measured local EEF z is not height above the tabletop. There is no supplied depth, object pose or camera calibration. Only RGB and measured robot EEF/gripper/base state are available. Base observed yaw is radians; tool command yaw is degrees. You have one continuous conversation and may choose any continuous EEF target, both arms, base motion and waypoint batching. Use execute_eef sequentially, not parallel calls. Use observations to judge motion and object manipulation. The server determines task success independently. Do not start another episode. Resource limits: unlimited tool calls, physics deadline enforced by the server; no hard token limit; resource reminders start after 2,000,000 cumulative tokens. Resource reminders ask you to reduce unnecessary token use while continuing useful work. Select only useful camera views after the initial observation to control resource use. When finished, state the observed outcome concisely.\nYou may call execute_wam to delegate a manipulation segment to a learned policy. Choose when to delegate using the current scene. It replans every 10 steps and automatically returns control after the requested segment (at most 300 steps). The WAM prompt is fixed to this task's instruction; always pass instruction=null and never invent a per-call prompt. Inspect its returned RGB/state and take over with EEF waypoints or delegate again. Use your own waypoint planning for approach, coordination and recovery as useful. The learned policy uses the same current three RGB cameras plus robot state, and its own frames from earlier in this episode; it receives no object ground truth or demonstration images.\nManipulation guidance: after visually aligning, close at a stationary pose and allow settling. A nonzero finger gap on the table is contact evidence, not proof of a secure grasp. Test each grasp by lifting only 0.01 m first, using motion_steps=16 or more, then hold 8..16 steps and request the active wrist plus overlook views. Keep orientation and base fixed during this test. Check both finger gap and RGB to confirm the object rises with the gripper before further lifting or transport. If it remains on the table or slips, lower and adjust local contact depth/centering using current images. There is no fixed limit on failed grasp attempts; retry or change approach within the remaining experiment budgets. Avoid large instantaneous target jumps while holding an object; use motion_steps for controlled transport. Total steps per waypoint must remain <=64 and includes the ramp. Use only the active wrist for local corrections, adding overlook when establishing alignment, testing grasp, or verifying placement.\nRGB inputs are downsampled: overlook 640x360, wrist 384x288; no crop or object annotation.\nThe saved ICL demonstration remains available through query_icl throughout this episode. Revisit it when the target region, stage order or arm roles are unclear, and when repeated failures or a change of approach make the reference useful. Use view=text for the original annotation and keyframe index, view=frames with frame_indices for selected images, or view=all for the full reference. Indices are zero-based entries in the keyframe index, not source video frame numbers. Querying does not advance simulation. Ground actions in the current RGB/state.",
